<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!-- Bugtopia interop generator — drives BepInEx's own Cpp2IL/Il2CppInterop pipeline OUT OF PROCESS,
       so the Il2Cpp interop assemblies can be regenerated after a game update without launching the
       game at all. See docs/plans/2026-08-27-bepinex-injector.md §4.2.

       WHY IT WORKS: BepInEx's `Il2CppInteropManager.Initialize()` is two separable halves —
       `GenerateInteropAssemblies()` (Cpp2IL over GameAssembly.dll + global-metadata, then the
       generator; pure file I/O) and `Il2CppInteropRuntime.Create(...).Start()` (needs a live IL2CPP
       runtime). We call only the first. Verified by decompiling BepInEx 6.0.0-be.785.

       WHY PURE REFLECTION, NO COMPILE-TIME REFERENCES: the three entry points we need
       (`Il2CppInteropManager.GenerateInteropAssemblies`, `.ComputeHash`, `UnityInfo.Initialize`) are
       private/internal, so reflection is forced anyway. Going all the way means this project builds
       on any machine with no BepInEx install present, needs no HintPaths, and — importantly — has no
       typerefs that the JIT could try to resolve before our AssemblyLoadContext probe is installed.

       TFM is net6.0 to match the CoreCLR that BepInEx itself ships (6.0.7). That is not cosmetic:
       it lets this assembly be loaded into BepInEx's own runtime by a host that has no .NET of its
       own - which is how a native launcher can drive generation without carrying a runtime. A net8.0
       consumer references it fine in the meantime.

       Build: dotnet build launcher/BugtopiaInterop/BugtopiaInterop.csproj -c Release -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net6.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>disable</Nullable>
    <AssemblyName>BugtopiaInterop</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>Bugtopia.Interop</RootNamespace>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>false</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
